---
/*
 * The third floating object: a galaxy, not a music note.
 *
 * The brief was "a clump of something, on black", and at 54px that is the
 * only kind of astronomy that survives — a black hole or a nebula would both
 * collapse into a smudge. A barred spiral keeps its silhouette at any size:
 * bright core, two arms, dark sky.
 *
 * The arms are real logarithmic spirals (r = a·e^(b·θ), squashed and tilted
 * so the disc reads as a plate seen at an angle) generated once and frozen
 * into the path data — nothing is computed at runtime.
 *
 * It does NOT turn, and that is deliberate. The disc used to rotate once every
 * 72s, which looked right on its own and wrong in the row: the rocket and the
 * dinosaur beside it are still PNGs, so the one moving badge read as the only
 * finished one rather than as the liveliest. Either all three move or none do,
 * and none is the cheaper promise to keep.
 *
 * Bottom left, three aligned stars: Orion's belt. It is the one thing in the
 * hero a pupil can point at and say they have seen it.
 *
 * Black sphere, warm-gold core — so the badge still balances the black note
 * it replaces and rhymes with the rocket's nose cone.
 */
